{"name":"Washed Dollar","hex":"#e1e3d7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"washed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of wash"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Ellipsis of washed up."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/washed"},{"term":"dollar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Official designation for currency in some parts of the world, including Canada, the United States, Australia, New Zealand, Singapore, Hong Kong, and elsewhere. Its symbol is $.","Money generally."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dollar"}],"entries":[{"title":"Washed","blocks":[{"text":"\"Washed\" is a song recorded live by the American Christian music collective Elevation Rhythm. The song was released on September 19, 2025, through Elevation Worship Records, to digital download and streaming formats. It was written by Joe L. Barnes, Joshua Holiday, Mitch Wong, and Steven Furtick, while Holiday produced. \"Washed\" peaked at number 97 on the Billboard Hot 100 and number 1 on the Hot Christian Songs chart."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/6c/Elevation_Rhythm_Washed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washed"},{"title":"Dollar","blocks":[{"text":"Dollar is the name of more than 25 currencies and their base units. The United States dollar, named after the international currency known as the Spanish dollar, was established in 1792 and is the first so named that still survives. Others include the Australian dollar, Brunei dollar, Canadian dollar, Eastern Caribbean dollar, Hong Kong dollar, Jamaican dollar, Liberian dollar, Namibian dollar, New Taiwan dollar, New Zealand dollar, Singapore dollar, Trinidad and Tobago dollar, and several others. The symbol for most of those currencies is the dollar sign $; the same symbol is used by many countries using peso currencies."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ad/Joachimsthaler_1525.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dollar"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Dollar_du_Sarawak.jpg/500px-Dollar_du_Sarawak.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Dollar_du_Sarawak.jpg/500px-Dollar_du_Sarawak.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One Sarawak dollar from 1935, featuring Charles Vyner Brooke, the 3rd and last White Rajah of Sarawak","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADollar_du_Sarawak.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Philip_V_Coin_silver%2C_8_Reales_Mexico.jpg/500px-Philip_V_Coin_silver%2C_8_Reales_Mexico.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Philip_V_Coin_silver%2C_8_Reales_Mexico.jpg/500px-Philip_V_Coin_silver%2C_8_Reales_Mexico.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Spanish dollar, natively called Peso, was the main coin of the Spanish Empire. This coin is from 1739.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhilip_V_Coin_silver%2C_8_Reales_Mexico.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/27/Nz_%241_front.jpg/250px-Nz_%241_front.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/27/Nz_%241_front.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/27/Nz_%241_front.jpg/250px-Nz_%241_front.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A New Zealand one-dollar coin","link":"https://en.wikipedi